Guided Exercise: Manage Long-lived and Short-lived Applications
Goal of the Exercise
You will deploy two types of workloads:
Workload	Resource Used
Database server (long-running)	Deployment
Batch application (short-lived)	Job
You will learn how to:
•	Create deployments
•	Fix pod failures using environment variables
•	Create and run jobs
•	View logs of jobs
•	Understand pod templates in job resources
________________________________________
Step 1 — Start the Lab
Command:
lab start deploy-workloads
Purpose
This command:
•	prepares the OpenShift cluster
•	ensures required resources are available
•	resets the lab environment
________________________________________
Step 2 — Login to OpenShift
Command:
oc login -u developer -p developer https://api.ocp4.example.com:6443
Explanation
Part	Meaning
oc	OpenShift CLI
login	authenticate to cluster
-u developer	username
-p developer	password
URL	API server address
________________________________________
Step 3 — Create a New Project
Command:
oc new-project deploy-workloads
What is a Project?
In OpenShift:
Project = Kubernetes Namespace + access control
Projects help:
•	isolate applications
•	organize resources
•	manage permissions
________________________________________
Step 4 — Create a MySQL Deployment
Command:
oc create deployment my-db \
--image registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/rhel9/mysql-80:1
Explanation
Part	Meaning
create deployment	create a deployment resource
my-db	deployment name
--image	container image used
This deployment tries to run a MySQL database server.
________________________________________
Step 5 — Check Deployment Status
Command:
oc get deployments
Example output:
NAME    READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE
my-db   0/1     1            0
Meaning
The deployment created a pod but it is not ready.
________________________________________
Step 6 — Check Pod Status
Command:
oc get pods
Example output:
my-db-xxxxx   CrashLoopBackOff
CrashLoopBackOff Meaning
The container:
•	starts
•	crashes
•	Kubernetes restarts it
•	crashes again
So it loops continuously.
________________________________________
Step 7 — Check Pod Logs
Command:
oc logs deploy/my-db
Output shows:
You must specify:
MYSQL_USER
MYSQL_PASSWORD
MYSQL_DATABASE
or MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD
Reason for Failure
The MySQL container requires environment variables.
Without them the container cannot start.
________________________________________
Step 8 — Fix Deployment (Add Environment Variables)
Command:
oc set env deployment/my-db \
MYSQL_USER=developer \
MYSQL_PASSWORD=developer \
MYSQL_DATABASE=sampledb
Explanation
Part	Meaning
set env	add environment variables
deployment/my-db	target deployment
variables	database configuration
These variables configure MySQL:
MYSQL_USER
MYSQL_PASSWORD
MYSQL_DATABASE
________________________________________
Step 9 — Verify Deployment Works
Command:
oc get deployments
Output:
NAME   READY   AVAILABLE
my-db  1/1     1
Meaning:
The pod is running successfully.
________________________________________
Step 10 — Get Pod IP Address
Command:
oc get pods -o wide
Example output:
NAME                STATUS    IP
my-db-xxxxxx        Running   10.8.0.91
-o wide
Shows additional information:
•	IP address
•	node
•	container details
________________________________________
Step 11 — Store Pod IP in Variable
Command:
DB_IP=$(oc get pods my-db-xxxxx \
-o=jsonpath='{.status.podIP}')
What this does
Stores pod IP in variable:
$DB_IP
Used later to connect to database.
________________________________________
Step 12 — Test MySQL Server
Command:
oc run -it db-test --restart=Never \
--image registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/rhel9/mysql-80:1 \
-- mysql sampledb -h $DB_IP -u developer --password=developer \
-e "select 1;"
Explanation
Part	Meaning
oc run	create temporary pod
-it	interactive terminal
--restart=Never	run once
mysql	MySQL client
select 1	test query
Output:
| 1 |
| 1 |
Meaning:
Database server works correctly.
________________________________________
Step 13 — Delete the Pod
Command:
oc delete pod -l app=my-db
What happens?
Deployment controller detects:
replicas < desired replicas
So Kubernetes creates a new pod automatically.
________________________________________
Step 14 — Verify Pod Recreation
Command:
oc get pod -l app=my-db
Output:
my-db-newpod   Running
Meaning:
Deployment self-healing works.
________________________________________
Step 15 — Create a Job
Now we deploy a short-lived batch workload.
Command:
oc create job date-loop \
--image registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/ubi9/ubi \
-- /bin/bash -c "for i in {1..30}; do date; done"
Explanation
Part	Meaning
create job	create batch job
date-loop	job name
image	container image
script	command executed
Script:
for i in {1..30}
do
date
done
Meaning:
Print date 30 times.
________________________________________
Step 16 — Inspect Job YAML
Command:
oc get job date-loop -o yaml
Important parts:
Command
command:
- /bin/bash
- -c
- for i in {1..30}; do date; done
Image
image: ubi9/ubi
Restart Policy
restartPolicy: Never
Meaning:
Job pod runs once and exits.
________________________________________
Step 17 — Check Job Status
Command:
oc get jobs
Output:
NAME       COMPLETIONS
date-loop  1/1
Meaning:
Job completed successfully.
________________________________________
Step 18 — View Job Logs
Command:
oc logs job/date-loop
Example output:
Fri Nov 18 14:50:56
Fri Nov 18 14:50:59
Fri Nov 18 14:51:01
Logs show date printed repeatedly.
________________________________________
Step 19 — Delete Job Pod
Command:
oc delete pod -l job-name=date-loop
Deletes the pod created by job.
________________________________________
Step 20 — Verify Pod is NOT Recreated
Command:
oc get pod -l job-name=date-loop
Output:
No resources found
Why?
Because:
Job already completed
Kubernetes does not restart completed jobs.
________________________________________
Step 21 — Check Job Status
Command:
oc get job -l job-name=date-loop
Output:
date-loop   1/1
Meaning:
Job status remains completed.
________________________________________
Step 22 — Finish the Lab
Command:
lab finish deploy-workloads
Purpose:
•	clean resources
•	reset cluster environment
________________________________________
Key Concepts Learned
Deployment (Long-Lived Workload)
Features:
•	manages pods continuously
•	self-healing
•	maintains replica count
Example:
Web servers
APIs
Databases
________________________________________
Job (Short-Lived Workload)
Features:
•	runs once
•	completes and stops
•	not restarted after success
Example:
backups
data processing
batch scripts
________________________________________
Deployment vs Job
Feature	Deployment	Job
Runs continuously	✅	❌
Self-healing	✅	❌
One-time task	❌	✅
Pod restart after success	✅	❌
